7. Words and Archetypes

I cannot doubt that language owes its origin to the imitation and modification, aided by signs and gestures, of various natural sounds, the voices of other animals, and man's own instinctive cries.  
(Charles Darwin, The Descent of Man)

Cratylus says that there is a correctness of name for each thing, one that belongs to it by nature. A thing's name isn't whatever people agree to call it—some bit of their native language that applies to it—but there is a natural correctness to names, which is the same for everyone, Greek or foreigner. 
(Plato, Cratylus)

At some point, relatively recently, we began to speak, or more correctly we started to use words. But, to be clear, memories, emotions, archetypes, ideas, were already boiling inside us communicating in its own strange language from within. Now I said words are code that are used to instantiate ideas in the mind. While I take this to be correct, it is somewhat of an oversimplification. Words are also used to express emotions and share memories. (We also use words to ask questions, alert others, issue commands, etc. But I am not concerned with that here.) The very first proto-words were likely used for expressing emotions. When such articulation was possible our ancestors can be taken (even if as a reaction) to have routinely vocalized: "ah," "ow," "ooh," "ick," "huh," "poof," "ha ha," "ugh," "aw," "eek," etc., and other expressions of the sort. I take these, which we now call interjections, to be more than mere sounds. "Ha ha," for instance, would have had significance to our ancestors. It would have been understood to relay an emotion like joy or excitement. And it seems the first intentional words spoken for joy or happiness, for example, would include the sound "ha." (Though I concede, I am not pursuing a linguistic analysis. It is nearly impossible to trace any known languages to the original proto-language(s). I instead speculate on how a proto-language could naturally arise, given some of the other concepts I endorse.) Our ancestors, as Darwin points out, likely were also able to mimic calls/sounds of animals and other noises in the environment. So there seemed to be a reservoir of sounds to draw phonemes from. The big question, of course, would be how these phonemes, once words, become language?  But I am foremost in this section concerned with the naturalness of words.
	The issue of the naturalness of words in language was first raised by Plato in the Cratylus. The question in the dialogue is whether there is a fit or appropriateness to the words we use, or instead that words are arbitrary and simply a matter of convention? Plato is somewhat ambivalent on the matter. But I take it there are arguments that might be siphoned in support of the view that words (names) are not merely conventional (without necessarily taking the extreme naturalist position of Cratylus in the dialogue. ) In looking to naturalness, the first words were likely onomatopoeias, where the word directly imitates the sound of the thing it describes. For example, the word "snore" imitates the sound we make in our sleep and the word "clap" imitates the sound our hands make when we strike them together. Other examples would be words like "roar" which mimics the animal sound it describes and "drip-drop" which mimics the sound of slowly falling water. (Many signs in sign language also manually mimic the words they describe.) The link between the word and the sound in these types have appropriateness and are not arbitrary, though there are admittedly limitations to their complexity and explanatory power. 
	In the dialogue, Plato suggests that appropriateness or fit can be derived from the intentions of the original rule-setter or name-giver.  He takes the name giver(s) to have been wise and something like a philosopher or dialectician. Such a person, we take it, would not arbitrarily name things, but instead would assign names for words in a way that reflects their nature, or at minimum captures some likeness. For example, I would say in naming something such as a 'chair', the chosen word should reflect certain attributes, for instance, like having legs, a base, being low, used for sitting, or for leisure, etc. To use a rather straightforward example, the English word "chair" can be traced back to the Greek word "kathedra" which can be broken down into the roots "kata" (down) and "hedra" (seat). So, this example seems an appropriate and fitting name, in that it reflects 'sitting' and 'being low'. Of course, not all examples are this clean. But this captures what a fitting word might look like.   
	Plato also holds appropriateness can be derived from phonemes themselves. He takes certain types of pronunciations to imitate the things named.  For instance, he claims, using the letter "r" causes some agitation in its pronunciation and is best used to describe things in motion or hardness. Pronouncing "i" involves the expulsion of breath and is best used in describing small things. In pronouncing "l" the tongue glides suggesting softness and smoothness. Pronouncing "g" stops the gliding of the tongue and should be used to describe something intense. Pronouncing "n" brings the tongue inward being suggestive of things that are within or enclosed. The phonemes "a" and "ē" are pronounced long being used for largeness or length. The letter "o" is taken to literally correspond to its shape and best used in words describing round things. We can of course question Plato's conclusions here, and there would be significant variation in different languages. But there do seem to be some sounds that are more pleasing and more fitting in describing certain things. The word "rock," for instance, doesn't quite seem like it would be an appropriate replacement for the word "daffodil," in referring to the type of flower. If this is more than a gut feeling then we can say that words, at least following this type of rubric, are not entirely arbitrary. 
	But while I take these arguments to be convincing, I believe they only work in an already established linguistic community. I am interested instead in how the original proto-language(s) emerged in the absence of any phonemes or words. In pursuing this task, I do hold that there was a rule-setter or name/sound-giver. But, originally, I take this individual to be a singer rather than a philosopher or dialectician. I redraw on the singing ape hypothesis.  That is, in the pre-linguistic condition, after certain evolutionary developments, our ancestors took a liking to song. Considering song's strong emotional appeal this should have been something that captivated our ancestors or got their attention. I postulate that the best singer(s) would have been something like clan celebrities. Other clansman would have gathered around to listen to them. In accord, there would be strong incentive for the singer to experiment with different sounds and combinations of sounds in pursuit of novelty and effectiveness (at drawing out emotional responses).  Importantly, listening to the singer could be done without cost. There need not be any investment in listening to songs, compared to, for instance, heeding or not heeding a warning call. In this way, new sounds could freely be introduced to the clan; in which it seems likely other clansman would go on to try to imitate. Eventually, through a selective process, I hypothesize, this could produce spoken words or the progenitors of spoken words. Where, I hold, words are more robust (than mere sounds) and would have been more readily preserved once introduced. In the background, of course, there would also need to be an evolutionary shift (and probably sexual selection) favoring increased articulacy. Though I seek primarily to offer an explanation for the availableness of words.    
	In getting back to naturalness or fittingness, we might return to the ways in which I claimed words are used. I said words are code for instantiating ideas and then went on to claim words can be used to express emotions. It may now be useful to talk about a third important function of words: sharing memories. This need not be too complicated for simple memories. I might use words, for example, to say to a friend: "Just think of the bottle of champagne that exploded at the beach house last summer." Here the words are apt if the memory is shared and brought up in the other person's mind (in a more or less accurate representation). What's important to note is that an idea is typically not instantiated in the process of recollection, unless the recipient shifts to examining the individual components of the memory or the content of what was said. But usually that's not the case. The memory is simply brought up and re-experienced. On the other hand, the more sophisticated memories: archetypes, I take it, are rooted in a long-established natural process and can be used to act as a measure for determining which words are fit in a non-superficial way. 
	We've looked at a relatively small number of archetypes so far. But as Jung points out, there are "as many archetypes as there are typical situations in life."  We can thus rely on large numbers of archetypes to determine a word's fittingness in various usages and then extend it to similar instances. Though I'll try to stick to a few familiar archetypes. By doing so we can have an additional standard for showing that certain words are appropriate and fitting rather than arbitrary. But we might ask what gives archetypes authority in deciding which words are appropriate? For one, archetypes are universal and instinctual. They have a feel to them. Certain sounds and words dispose us to respond in a certain way. 
	Some of these feels would be to place archetypes into linguistic groupings such as masculine/feminine (i.e., Sword/Flower), positive/negative (i.e., Light/Dark), and lofty/banal (i.e., Breath/Mud). Feminine, positive, and lofty words should have a soft sound and be more drawn out. Masculine, negative, and banal words should sound harsher and be more abrupt. Though there are many exceptions to this. These groupings also determine what should be said about certain things. Words used to describe the Hero archetype and its derivations should not be feminine or negative. Words used to describe the Wise Old Man archetype and its derivations should not be banal. Words used in association with the Body, Blood, Bread archetypes should not be lofty, and so on. When a word deviates too much from its associative archetypal grouping it is no longer appropriate and becomes unfit. But one might object that these associations have no real foundation. The reply is that these linguistic feels along with its archetypes are ultimately rooted in human emotion. Emotions, being original existences, have no further explanation. We are simply disposed to respond to certain types of things in characteristic ways.
	To see an example of this, we might look at the Maiden archetype. It is not by chance that the word "maiden" is used (in English), rather than some other word. I take it the word "maiden" is appropriate and fit for its object. But the very similar word "broad," which means roughly the same thing (in reference to a young woman), is not. The word "broad," for instance, has a hard sound and is slightly negative, being inconsistent with the typical archetypal form. In rooting the word "broad" it can be traced to the Old English "brād," which means wide. It is not exactly clear how 'wideness' shares a likeness with the thing described; which instead should have characteristics like youth, beauty, being unmarried, female, a virgin, playfulness, desirability. One interpretation of the word "broad" is that 'wideness' refers to 'wide hips', suggesting female sexual appeal. But that specific distinction is not within the word itself and is instead simply stipulated as a matter of convention. Thus, "broad" is not an appropriate or fit word for capturing its referent—at least according to the standards we are applying.
	We might also rely on various archetypes and their meanings to ground appropriate likenesses in words. Considering this allows for connections to be derived from symbolic and metaphorical associations, a rich supply of word options become available to us. (We might also use proper names to draw associations, but this, in my opinion, is subject to too much variation, and is less apt than using archetypes.) For example, if we take 'machine' along with the symbolic and metaphorical associations in its archetype, we can create new (appropriate) words. Instead of relying solely on its root "mekhane," meaning a device or tool, we can add its symbolic aspects like body, ugliness, coldness, to it. For consideration, if we combined the root of ugliness, "uggr" (dreaded) with "mekhos" (have power) we might derive a new word capturing something like 'dreaded power of a machine' or a 'type of machine with dreaded power'. Considering there are in fact such machines on the horizon, which will be dreaded as well as powerful, this might provide a name for it that is fit, compared to one that is arbitrary or merely conventional. (Though, to be clear, a conventional name still works given people are told what it means and agree to use it in that way. And this is true of all conventional names. But it would lack naturalness.) 
	But how did archetypes become a linguistic standard? Let us return to our primitive linguistic community and jump forward in time. The clan is now a large tribe, and speaking one or two words at a time is relatively common. There are still singers, but a new type of tribal celebrity is emerging: the poet storyteller. He's met with indifference at first, but tribesman listen to his words, mainly because it costs nothing. Like the singer, the poet storyteller would have strong incentive to experiment with new speech patterns. But instead of sounds, his craft involves strings of words. He would try out different combinations of words in the pursuit of novelty and garnishing a favorable reaction from other tribesmen. The poet storyteller's words would initially lack the emotional force of a song. Until by chance, he fortuitously hits the right combination of words capable of invoking an archetype. Once this happens, tribesman would be captivated and drawn in, encouraging the poet storyteller to repeat the magic words for them. Emotional appeal could then be established—through archetypes—in many ways more powerful than what was possible with song. 
	From here, I take it, there would be the phenomenon of runaway loquaciousness. That is, after hearing the magic words—capable of capturing the archetypes within—there would be an insatiable impulse to rehear these words and repeat them to others. Once archetypes had the capacity to be shared externally, we would be compelled to continuously realize them, even when it does not directly aid our survival to do so—though it would work to the benefit of archetypes, and also eventually ideas. Thus, constant chatter will prevail. What's important is that speech be exorbitant and wasteful enough to produce enough material to allow for selective pressure. Archetypes bring this about. Rationality, I take it, in its intentionality, would also rapidly speed up the process of language acquisition once excessive talking became a consistent behavioral pattern. These things combined, I suggest, would lead to beneficial adaptations. I admit, these postulations are highly speculative, but at some point in our evolution, whatever that point may be, humans did in fact become pathological talkers, and still are to this day. I am just assigning archetypes as the cause.  
	In finishing up the quodlibet, I hold that there is a threshold where languages will start to change on their own. Once the threshold is crossed, a language without serious impediment will increase in complexity—assuming there are enough active speakers and continuity. I hold that archetypes gave the first push needed to cross the barrier.  But to be clear, we are still at the level of a proto-language. Languages don't reach their full potential until ideas are being exchanged on a large scale. It is ideas which carry language most effectively. Also, worth noting, is that I have said nothing about syntax. How strings of words can morph into syntactical language is its own unique puzzle. I am tempted to bring in Noam Chomsky's universal grammar to make it work.  But even Chomsky largely rejects universal grammar these days.  My tempered response would be that rationality includes an aesthetic faculty that can somehow choose, whether it's based on mathematics, or music, or rhythm, or harmony, whatever. Some things simply seem more beautiful or more harmonious than others. And fluency in some ways resembles this: certain combinations of words just don't sound right; and others do. This may prove dissatisfying to some. So I leave it to the linguists to connect words to grammar.  
	Towards the end of the Cratylus, Plato claims that we should perhaps not focus on words but rather on knowing the essential nature of things (mainly, his theory of Forms).  But we may ask, in regard to essential natures, what it is that we contemplate in the absence of words? Is it an undifferentiated mental image, or instead, some feeling of knowing? A particular memory might also be a possible option if we take Plato's theory of Recollection seriously.  But this doesn't quite seem right. I would say words are required to truly make sense of Forms—if Forms are possible—or ideas in general, and that there is a linguistic Form-naturalism that can be had. For instance, if we take Plato's Good simply to be or included in the aesthetic faculty, it might act as a mechanism in which to see whether words are appropriate or fit their object. Where discovering this appropriateness or fittingness reveals knowledge of the object. Plato does comment that "naming things (kaloun) is to perform beautiful (kalon) works."  How seriously to take this is a matter of debate. Counter to what Plato claims—in the Cratylus at least—words do act as a means through which we acquire knowledge and meaning. 


8. Language and Meaning

Words exist because of meaning. Once you've gotten the meaning, you can forget the words. Where can I find a man who has forgotten words so I can have a word with him. 
(Zhuang Zhou, Zhuangzi)

So soon as I talk I express the universal, and if I do not do so, no one can understand me.
(Søren Kierkegaard, Fear and Trembling)

In the previous section, I told a story of the genesis of words. The truth is we'll never know for sure how words originally came to be. But if I'm allowed a certain latitude, I think we can respond to an ongoing problem in philosophy: linguistic meaning. I want to be clear that linguistic meaning is something different than Meaning in general or something like The Meaning of Life. Unfortunately, I don't have those answers. Though I hope to offer a satisfactory answer to spiritual meaning by the end of the essay. To get at linguistic meaning, we must delve into the nature of language. This will take some sorting out. 
	To start, I claimed that words are primarily code for instantiating ideas—as well as for expressing emotions and sharing memories. My focus was largely on spoken words. But to be clear, words need not be vocal. Along with written letters (representing sounds), I take certain hand gestures and pictures and mathematical symbols to be words or serve the same function as words. I will limit myself to the first use of words: instantiating ideas. This is not to say the other two uses are unimportant. But limiting myself in this way will make the argument much more manageable. One thing to clarify, is that while all languages are composed of words, words are not sufficient for language in itself. What is it then that makes words (individually or as an aggregate) language or encompass language? 
	The basic answer, and subject of this section, is that for a word or words to count as language they must carry or convey meaning. There are of course ways of communicating without this requirement being met, such as shouting interjections, but usually we wouldn't call it language. Or it could not function in itself as a language. Though this requirement could be challenged. If language does require meaning, which I take to be the case, then we might raise the following questions: What does linguistic meaning correspond to (besides the words themselves)? What makes words meaningful? What is the content of meaning-imparting words? And how are these meaning-imparting words effectively communicated? These questions will shape the discussion that follows. Though I hope to do a little more than simply answer these questions.
	Jumping right in, I hold that meaning does not correspond to reality but rather to ideas. But this is not to take the Lockean position that meaning is whatever particular mental image the word references.  Rather, meaning is found in the configuration. And to reiterate, we all manifest the same configuration of each idea. In fact, it would be difficult if not impossible to convey meaning with one another if there wasn't some shared configuration to draw from. One might counter that we can share meaning through similar instances or like-enough mental images. But this, I believe, would be an appeal to memories not ideas. We can indeed elicit like memories in others through the right words—and as previously stated this is one use of words. But this is more shared familiarity than shared meaning. (Though emotional significance might be established, and relayed, which we might confuse with meaning.) 
	I don't want to completely discount the phenomenon in question. As I pointed out, most of our interaction with ideas is through mental images; mental images being memories under my view. So, when making sense of an idea like 'chair' we will typically utilize mental images of individual chairs drawn from memory. But while it may be possible to elucidate "chairness" by pointing to several individual chairs, in memory, or in the world, this only establishes resemblance; and possibly difference once we try to categorize it in the mind. While this is a useful process, and in many cases unavoidable, it is not necessary for meaning. The meaning in the configuration can be had without the flurry of particular mental images. Though in practice particular mental images will present along with it. This is not entirely problematic, except for the fact that most people take the mental images themselves to be the idea; which isn't the case. 
	In reply to Master Zhuang, there may be an even more peculiar man who has forgotten both words and particular mental images. What remains? It seems what is preserved are abstract concepts. Though it is unclear how abstract concepts persist. But we seem to have some experience of it. What are abstract concepts? They might be taken as some shared aspect that can be attributed to like particular things, but is not a particular itself. In language, adjectives are commonly the mechanism used to attach or instill abstract concepts to particular things. Typically, adjectives are properties that a thing might have. But we may ask what the adjective contributes to the particular that the particular doesn't already possess? Or likewise, we may question whether adjectives represent real properties or are redundant? This creates a problem, which might be called the problem of adjectives. Mainly, language doesn't necessarily need adjectives as they are typically used to convey meaning. This seems like a small claim, but it has huge ramifications if we take it seriously.  For example, instead of saying "red chair" we could instead say "chairness and redness occur together" or interchangeably "redness and chairness occur together." All that is needed is the primitive idea of Time, and maybe Proximity to make sense of this. And it is a perfectly viable means of linguistic communication.
	Of course, coherently taking a view like this forces us down some interesting paths, in some ways resembling the historical Problem of Universals debate , mainly: (1) universals—abstract concepts—are real and this is just the manifestation of two unique universals, chairness and redness, in the world within their particular substratum (material makeup) , or (2) there is only a single underlying undifferentiated substratum and all other descriptions, including chairness and rednesss, are universal predicates of it , or (3) there are really only universals, such as chairness and redness, and no necessary substratum—e.g., idealism; and if the presentation is faulty or imperfect or a copy, Platonic idealism, or (4) there are only particular things, substratum or otherwise, and chairness and redness are just arbitrary descriptions we give to them (viz, they are not universals) , or (5) there are indeed lots of unique particular things, like red chairs, but when we try to categorize seemingly like things in the mind we create something entirely new and distinct: the universal.  Or my view (6) that there is a preexisting configuration for every possible type, such as chairness and redness, which we might loosely call the universal, but the universal only exists potentially, not actually, unless instantiated in a mind. Of course, one odd implication of my view is that if you already have the words, as code, you don't need to rely on particular things. But, to be clear, you also need physical memories, like seeing furniture or colors, which require particular things at some point. So it is a type of dualism in practice.  Unsurprisingly, I subscribe to (6). But my theory of linguistic meaning could work with any of the options given, considering that meaning corresponds to ideas not reality. Why does this matter? 
	If we drop adjectives as modifiers we are forced to explain properties and universals. We cannot simply pivot to the noun or object. While a red chair is mundane, chairness and redness are mysterious. It is likely because of the widespread use of adjectives that there are many abstract concepts that we simply cannot explain or account for. If this weren't the case, we would have derived meaningful explanations for these things over the last several thousand years—out of necessity. This is not to say the stuff of adjectives don't exist. They clearly do. But they are misunderstood. What to do? I say treat them on their own, rather than relationally. Here we might stop talking about red chairs and instead talk about chairness and redness. If we can form a coherent idea of chairness and redness we can easily understand red chairs. But the reverse isn't true. In searching for linguistic meaning, I propose we should engage universals themselves, as ideas, rather than haphazardly attaching them to particulars or classes of particulars. For instance, chairs need not be red. Once we accept this, we will be in a much better semantic position—at least in regard to universals, however they might be defined. In fact, dealing with ideas or universals, rather than particulars, is where we will find the substance of linguistic meaning—as opposed to mere familiarity or conventional agreement. 
	In rebuttal, one could argue that we lose useful descriptions without adjectives, like large, small, rich, fat, tall. This is extremely easy to compensate for. Instead of "tall" we could just say "2 meters." This relays more information and avoids the inherent vagueness in such terms. The detractor may go on, what about deep, shallow, heavy, dim, bright? Again, we could just say "2 meters," or "100 kilograms," or "200 lumens." And then, finally, big ones, like beautiful, ugly, good, bad. I take it these are so important to warrant being treated in themselves, as beauty, goodness, etc., rather than being used as vague and confused adjectives. "The Good," being one application of this sentiment. It makes more philosophical sense to say the Good is or occurs rather than this or that thing is good. I'm not saying we necessarily need to make the adjustment put forward. It likely would be inefficient and impractical. I'm only saying such an adjustment is possible linguistically, and meaning could be relayed just fine, in some cases better. So with that, let's put adjectives aside in order to avoid a monumental labor of linguistics in the non-philosophical fashion. 
	The next question is what is transferred in the process of linguistic communication? In particular, what is necessary for linguistic meaning to be achieved? I said it was code, and that a certain type of code is needed to instantiate each idea. But just like in the case of programmable code, code as words can be neat or messy, as well as complex or wasteful. It is also the case that some ideas can be ran on a single word or a few words, whereas others may require hundreds or thousands or tens of thousands of words to run. Some people may also fail to successfully run ideas even in the presence of the right code. Others may be unable to run certain ideas at all, due to corruption in their software, or defective hardware, so to speak. But we might side-step this and ask what is the minimal amount of words, as code, needed to run an idea in a mind able to instantiate it? The linguistic light coal. This of course will vary based on the idea and to a small extent the spoken language. The idea of 'redness' may only require a single word to run (i.e., "red," "roja," "aka"), the idea of 'triangle' may require a few words to run ("three-sided figure"), and an idea like 'chair' will require many words to run, including a subroutine for the program 'furniture'. One might point out that we can establish the idea of triangle, for instance, by using names for different types of triangles. But, I take it, for these names to have any meaning a person must already have the idea of triangle in the mind. 
	For very complicated ideas there are programs within greater programs and so on, as I established earlier in the essay. There are also foundational ideas which all other ideas rely on, which I held to be primitive ideas. The primitive ideas cannot be run with words, so cannot be communicated to others. Though in some sense we have individual access to them if we are willing to search. But as I said, this is largely a mystical process. In terms of meaning, a person usually would like to relay a lot more than the linguistic light coal. Their personal perspective, as we might say. But due to the economy of language only a small portion of this mental package is typically delivered or hits. Hence, Kierkegaard is right to worry that something important is lost in communication. Largely, only the universal is transferred, in terms of linguistic meaning. Subjective meaning is mostly lost. But this need not be as significant of a worry as it seems. Because we all manifest the same universals, ideas, in the mind we can have some sense of what is going on in other peoples' heads. Archetypes and emotions are also similar for all humans. So, if we accept my greater theory, we share a lot of the same mental features with our fellow man. The only unique aspect that remains are simple memories. And perhaps this is what subjectivity is. Though, that being said, the interrelation of all of a person's individual ideas, emotions, and memories likely produces something novel that only the person themself can understand. There may also be bodily influences that we have no way of explaining. Thus, special subjectivity is still an issue, and something that must be sacrificed in the practice of language.
	But let us return to linguistic meaning itself. Linguistic meaning, as I claimed, is conveyed if the intended idea is instantiated in a mind capable of receiving it. When someone says I understand what you mean, they imply that they share the same idea or can identify the same idea that you are trying to relay. In my view, this is not just a proximation, but two people can in fact share identical ideas. So, there is a genuine sense in which meaning is relayed. And this is what I take linguistic meaning to be: the shared instantiation of ideas through words. It is worth considering that when people use superfluous words (messy or wasteful code) there may be accidental meaning that might be achieved other than the intended meaning. I don't take this to be linguistic meaning. It is rather misinterpretation. People can derive many things from superfluous words and superfluous variations of words, even gibberish, but if it is unintended, meaning is not exchanged but rather is simply an extension of already existing meaning in the listener. I do acknowledge hearing some nonsense or gibberish may incite some really profound thoughts. But this is not communication, and is entirely accidental. For instance, if you heard the same gibberish an hour before you likely wouldn't have those profound thoughts. If you would have those thoughts anyway, then any gibberish would likely do; though certain words may function as a stimulus. But a stimulus like this wouldn't contain linguistic meaning. That being said, accidental meaning is something to take seriously. And it may count against my theory. But at least in my view, linguistic meaning must be intentional and shared.
	In wrapping up linguistic meaning, I should add some caveats regarding the instantiation of ideas. Lista: People typically only instantiate some ideas, not all possible ideas. While not infinite, the range of possible ideas is vast. We may (personally or collectively as a species) currently lack the necessary code or words to instantiate some ideas. Some ideas may be beyond the reach of all existing languages. A new medium of communication, or a new category of words, may be needed to instantiate some ideas. The human brain may need to increase in complexity to process particularly complicated ideas requiring millions or billions of words to instantiate. There may be some ideas that are uninstantiable by merely organic beings. Some ideas may require some other reservoir besides a physical brain to instantiate them. And some ideas may not be instantiable at all. Thus, linguistic meaning—as the shared instantiation of ideas through words—is an evolving process and incomplete. 
	I would be remiss, in ending this topic, if I did not complete the story of our aspiring linguistic community. They are now part of a confederacy of tribes with a shared language. The language is still primitive and there are competing dialects amongst the tribes, but plentiful words are spoken and exchanged between them. A rudimentary syntax is also present. But there is a problem. Language continues to shift toward simplicity. Luckily (and grant me some leeway here for the sake of a good tale) the next verbal rectifier will appear: the epic storyteller. This is not to say people weren't telling stories. They were. But in this early stage, fiction was a new concept, as opposed to merely lying. Fiction will play an important role in our species' ascent to culture.  And the first individuals skilled at producing great oral fiction performed an invaluable service to the confederacy. Not to mention it was likely highly entertaining to listeners—promoting spread and adoption. A side-effect of this elaborate storytelling is that language and syntax become greatly improved. When every word choice is crucial and sentences are crafted specifically for the task of producing epics, language evolves, both in complexity and poetic appeal. Inevitably, competing epics would emerge amongst the tribes. One or more epic, I take it, over time would become dominant and gain authority in the confederacy. This type of linguistic and artistic selection then becomes paramount in defining and uniting communities. And from here we can move away from wild speculation to the typical practice of cultural anthropology. Thus, the next section takes us forward by exploring one of the first and most important derivations of your typical epic: myths. 
